. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H3 * (Please adapt according to your needs)
. 
.         * Please add a verbal description of your model here
.         
.         *We estimate two linear regression models to assess whether having da
> ughters is associated with greater gender equality in household behaviors. Th
> e dependent variables are perf2 and perf, which measure the within-couple dif
> ference in time spent on housework and childcare relatively to the annual ave
> rage, with positive values indicating greater involvement of men relative to 
> other couples. The key independent variable is has_daughter, a binary indicat
> or equal to 1 if the household has at least one daughter. We control for the 
> number of children (nb_kid), respondent's age and its square (age, age2), inc
> ome (hghinc), satisfaction with life (plh0182), and a set of individual-level
>  covariates including family status (pgfamstd), employment status (pgemplst),
>  and education level (pgpsbil). Year fixed effects (syear), gender (sex), reg
> ion (sampreg), and migration background (migback) are also included. Standard
>  errors are clustered at the individual level (pid) and robust to heteroskeda
> sticity.
. 
.         * The following models are tested
.         
. *reg perf2 has_daughter nb_kid age age2  plh0182 hghinc i.(pgfamstd pgpsbil p
> gemplst syear sex sampreg migback) if age>15 , cluster(pid) r
. 
. reg perf has_daughter nb_kid age age2  plh0182 hghinc i.(pgfamstd pgpsbil pge
> mplst syear sex sampreg migback) if age>15 & nb_kid>0, cluster(pid) r

